<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ancient Shu</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 316 BCE (approx.) · capital Chengdu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The early kingdom of the Sichuan basin, heir to the astonishing bronze culture of Sanxingdui, conquered by Qin in 316 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Shu emerged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> plain from a long local tradition: the Sanxingdui culture, with its giant bronze masks and standing figure, and the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinsha-site">Jinsha site</a> in Chengdu. Later legends name a series of kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cancong">Cancong</a>, Yufu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/du-yu-king-of-shu">Duyu</a>, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiming-dynasty">Kaiming dynasty</a>, but these accounts were written centuries afterwards.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Shu developed largely independently of the Zhou states, and its bronze art, sun worship and burial customs, including boat-shaped coffins, differ sharply from those of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiming-dynasty">Kaiming</a> kings it was centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> and was rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. It had no widely used script; the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-shu-script">Ba-Shu</a> signs remain undeciphered.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Quarrels between Shu and the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-ba">State of Ba</a> gave the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> a pretext; in 316 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-cuo">Sima Cuo</a> conquered Shu. Qin settled colonists there, and the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bing">Li Bing</a> built the Dujiangyan irrigation system, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> the granary that fuelled Qin's unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ancient-shu.jpg" alt="Ancient Shu" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:States_of_Zhou_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 316 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chengdu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shu local religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shu, State of Shu, Kaiming Shu</dd></dl></aside></div>

